Poll `/api/metrics` on an interval, no push. The platform kills WebSocket handshakes at the edge (501) and freezes instances between invocations, so SSE/WebSocket/long-lived subscriptions are off the table regardless of preference — this is a platform constraint, not a design choice.

Set the interval from the data's actual freshness, not from taste: `rollup` only changes once a minute (finance-close-driven, not shortenable), so polling faster than that just re-fetches byte-identical rows. Poll every 60s, aligned to the top of the minute with a few seconds of slack for the job to finish writing.

```js
async function load() { ... }  // unchanged
window.addEventListener('load', load);
document.getElementById('refresh').addEventListener('click', load);
setInterval(load, 60_000);
```

Depth left out: whether to skip the render when `computed_at` is unchanged (avoids a flicker if `render` isn't idempotent-safe), and whether to pause the interval on a hidden tab (`document.visibilityState`) to cut function invocations when nobody's looking.
